// What a request answered: the value it asked for, or the failure that kept it
// from arriving. Never both and never neither, which is why it is a payload
// enum rather than a struct of two options.
//
// An adapter returns its failure rather than raising it: a 404 is a response
// that arrived, and the request did not fail to run. A raise out of an adapter
// is a bug in the adapter, and reaches the nearest boundary like any other.
enum Result[T any] {
    ok     T
    failed Failure
}

// Why an attempt failed. `kind` is what a handler dispatches on, `message` is
// for a human, and `code` is the adapter's own.
//
// Not an `error`: that is raised and unwinds to the nearest `boundary`, where a
// failed request is a value a screen renders beside the stale answer it still
// has. A program that wants the boundary raises from `@fail`.
struct Failure {
    kind    FailureKind
    message string
    // The adapter's own code, verbatim: an HTTP status, a SQLSTATE, a gRPC
    // status name. Carried untouched, because only code that knows which
    // adapter produced it can read it.
    code    string
}

// The failure classes every adapter can produce, split by what a program
// should do next: retrying a `request` fails identically, retrying a `server`
// may work.
//
//   - `transport` -- never reached the peer
//   - `timeout` -- reached it, no answer in time
//   - `auth` -- a credential is missing, expired or rejected
//   - `request` -- the peer rejected what was sent
//   - `server` -- the peer failed on its own side
//   - `decode` -- the answer arrived and did not match the declared type
enum FailureKind { transport, timeout, auth, request, server, decode }

// A request bracketed by the tree: asked when the node mounts and whenever
// `on` changes, abandoned when it unmounts. `fetch` is the adapter's call, and
// it is handed the key it asks for.
//
// The three two-way props are independent facts, each a cell the call site
// binds or leaves to the instance:
//
//   - `value` -- the last answer, kept through a refresh and through a failure
//   - `pending` -- an attempt is in flight
//   - `failure` -- how the last attempt failed, cleared by a success
//
// **The latest attempt wins.** An answer to a key asked before the current one
// writes nothing, and nor does one arriving after the node unmounts, so a
// screen never shows what it is no longer asking about.
//
// It renders nothing, so it goes in any tree: a layout, a drawing or a menu.
component query[K any, T any, F any](
    // What the request is about. A change asks again.
    on       K,
    // The adapter's call, handed the key.
    fetch    func(K) Result[T],
    // The last answer.
    :value   option[T] = null,
    // An attempt is in flight.
    :pending bool = false,
    // How the last attempt failed.
    :failure option[Failure] = null,
    // An answer arrived.
    @load(value T),
    // An attempt failed.
    @fail(f Failure),
) F {
    var attempt = 0
    var refreshes = 0

    // Asks again without a change of key: a retry button, a poll from a
    // timer. The value stays, and `pending` says an attempt is in flight.
    func refresh() {
        refreshes += 1
    }

    effect(
        on = {key=on, n=refreshes},
        @mount(k) {
            attempt += 1
            var mine = attempt
            pending = true
            var r = fetch(k.key)
            if mine != attempt {
                return
            }
            pending = false
            match r {
                ok(var v) {
                    value = v
                    failure = null
                    load(v)
                }
                failed(var f) {
                    failure = f
                    fail(f)
                }
            }
        },
        @unmount { attempt += 1 },
    )
}
